  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs of 7 PM Monday evening, Surface cold front has now pushed \neastward into central New England. Aloft, the upper level trough\naxis is directly over the forecast area and will be quickly \nshifting eastward. Behind this departing storm system, all \nprecipitation has ended across the forecast area and drier air \nis starting to filter into the region thanks to breezy westerly \nwinds. Some gusts are in the 20-30 mph range, especially within\nthe Capital Region and Berkshires.  Dewpoints have already \nfallen into the lower 60s and they will continue to drop into \nthe 50s for tonight. The threat for thunderstorms and flash \nflooding has ended for today and temperatures will be more\ncomfortable for the overnight hours, with lows in the 50s.\n\nWinds will subside later tonight, with patchy fog developing \nlate, especially in sheltered areas and locations that saw \nsignificant rainfall.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMore tranquil weather is expected for Tue and also Wed, with \nhigh pressure in place. Humidity levels will be more comfortable\ncompared to today. Model guidance is indicating a very \nsmall/compact low moving onshore near SE New England, but latest\ntrends are for this system to remain south/east of our area. \n\nBy Thu, moisture and humidity will increase again as a \npersistent south-southwest flow develops around the periphery of\na Bermuda high. Dewpoints are expected to rise into the 70s, \nwith PWAT anomalies increasing to +1 to +2 STDEV. There does \nappear to be a lack of synoptic scale forcing through Thu \nmorning, as a frontal system is expected to be positioned \nnorth/west of Lake Ontario with no significant push to the south\ndue to the strengthening Bermuda high. Eventually the front \nshould slowly drift south/east into our area later Thu into Fri,\nwhich is when chances for showers/T-storms increase. Due to \nuncertainty in coverage/timing, there is low confidence in \nsevere storm and/or flash flood potential. AI NWP convective \nhazards forecast shows at least some potential for severe storms\nduring this time. Also, with such a humid/moist environment and\nwet antecedent ground conditions, there will likely be a threat\nof at least isolated flash flooding.",
